  "body": "Registered packages can declare proof inputs anywhere in the repository, but the workflow currently filters events before that declaration reaches the planner. A change confined to a declared certificate or review file can therefore skip replay. Two later selection gaps also lose required checks: Git's default rename detection reports only the new path, and a change to the replay workflow selects no packages.\n\nThis tooling repair closes those three gaps in the same invalidation path:\n\n- Run the planning job for otherwise eligible pull requests and pushes to `main`, without path filters. The manifest-aware selector still produces no replay jobs for unrelated changes.\n- Use `git diff --no-renames --name-only -z` so renaming an input invalidates consumers of its old path as well as its destination.\n- Treat the replay workflow, like the runner, as a shared control that selects every registered package.\n\nThe claim concerns packages present at the compared head, with canonical declared input paths and a valid comparison base. It does not infer undeclared imports or bypass GitHub event eligibility/permissions. Package verification, source pins, exactness checks, job timeouts and concurrency limits remain unchanged. No mathematical or \u03ba claim changes.\n\n**Concrete evidence:** pending PR1262 at `4b196b40dcac3ec40f62ec285babac53a1b5a3c5` declares 16 inputs. Four fall outside both former event filters: the selected certificate, the #1042 checkpoint, and the #1047/#1099 integration receipts. The base main tree has zero registered `VERIFY.json` files; this establishes a defect in the registration contract, not an observed missed hosted run. PR1262's mathematics is not evaluated here.\n\nValidation:\n\n```sh\npython3.12 -B -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_research_*.py' -v\n```\n\nExpected: **26 tests pass**. On the primary CPython 3.15.0 research runtime, **25 pass and one skips** because full generic replay explicitly requires 3.12. The compatibility run uses installed CPython 3.12.12. Python.org confirms 3.15.0 remains current stable.\n\n- Six new regression groups execute the actual planner CLI in temporary Git repositories. They cover inputs under certificates/docs/src, paths containing spaces, deletion, a Git-detected rename, both shared controls, unrelated edits, and new package-local artifacts. The JSON matrix and GitHub output count must agree.\n- Restoring only the original workflow and runner causes **three regression groups to fail**. A separate before/after Git-tree-object model, which neither calls `git diff` nor imports the planner, agrees on ten seed-94 scenarios, including shared consumers.\n- A separate Ruby YAML parser plus a bounded event model finds four excluded PR1262 inputs before the patch and none after. It is not a general Actions emulator. The no-path-filter regression is an explicit static policy guard.\n- **actionlint 1.7.12** passes for the changed workflow (`-shellcheck='' -pyflakes=''`). The selected-record check and all **25 selected-record tests** pass. No hosted CI execution, selected cold replay, Windows run, or independent human review is claimed.\n\nCost: the existing planning/helper-test job now runs on all otherwise eligible events, including unrelated edits. Expensive package jobs remain selective; changing the workflow deliberately replays all registered packages. Hosted startup cost was not measured.\n\nBase `bce54fb3f895cbe5cd4c0eb477c3afeed100190f`; OS seed **94**, verification engineering. Final overlap covered 834 open PRs, 76 discussion records, and 325 local records, with complete remote pagination and no warnings. No pinned coordination issue exists; the claim and evidence are saved locally. PR474 concerns a historical gen4 workflow. PR1298 and PR1299 change other launcher behavior; this adds a separate test file and does not include their pending fixes.\n\nCredit Douglas Colkitt for the package tooling and Sagar Thakrar for PR1262's concrete manifest example. Existing Apache-2.0 attribution is preserved. Prepared for Charles Bethin with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance in diagnosis, implementation, tests, and writing. The guide describes the revised contract. GitHub's documented path-filter behavior: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax#onpushpull_requestpull_request_targetpathspaths-ignore.\n",
